  "problem_context": {
    "problem_statement": "Migrate exactly cmc_write_byte from C*/HOL to QCP/Rocq. Source snapshot: /Users/nizitong/Documents/ChatGPT/Cmc/experiments/cmc_write_byte/baseline/cmc_concrete_emit_bytes.c and .h. Mathematical byte range is tower_byte_value z := 0<=z<256. Original C* models and helper proofs are available in baseline/cmc_rep.c, baseline/tower_base.c. Preserve C runtime signature and sole statement data[cursor] = byte. Preserve the semantic pre/post below; this is specification translation, not a request to weaken it or strengthen input assumptions.\nLogical parameters: prefix:list Z and model_byte:Z.\nPre: 0<=cursor<capacity; byte=model_byte; 0<=model_byte<256; ownership of initialized unsigned-byte prefix data[0..cursor) with exact contents prefix, separating-conjoined with ownership of uninitialized unsigned-byte suffix data[cursor..capacity).\nPost: ownership of initialized prefix data[0..cursor+1) with exact contents prefix ++ [model_byte], separating-conjoined with ownership of uninitialized suffix data[cursor+1..capacity). Preserve prefix contents and remaining footprint, do not replace this with only a point-store postcondition. No assumption that the entire array is initialized. No extra cursor/capacity/pointer restrictions beyond source semantics. QCP formal specification is to be authored by annotation owner from these semantics.\nUse existing QCP unsigned-char array predicates if equivalent; otherwise define reusable predicates and prove required lemmas in case lib, separately from case proof. Do not introduce new Axioms/Admitted to discharge manual obligations. Existing QCP auto-generated trusted admissions must be identified in report.\nThis is the first genuine C* -> QCP case for future CMC/TVM migration. Do not use the old reverse-direction arr_sum experiment as a proof template. Keep new common model/lemma work distinguishable from case annotation/proof for effort measurement. Record any semantic mismatch or unsupported byte-array feature explicitly. Follow controller roles and ownership.",
